At this time, the night was deep, and the world was silent. The slanted moon had already sunk, and there was no light or clarity. Between the darkness and gloom, while walking in the wild fields west of the room, he suddenly heard the sound of crying. It was faint, coming from far and then near. The listener also felt desolate and miserable. The scholar then hid in a secluded place to observe her movements. Just then, a beautiful woman arrived slowly. She walked while weeping. As he listened and watched, her fat and skin were moist and abundant; her appearance was enchanting, possessing a kind of beauty that startled the observer. She said repeatedly, "My good man, my good man, I do not know where you are now." The scholar was originally a man who lived without restraint, and when he saw her beauty, he felt joyful and rushed out before her. He teased her, saying, "Your good man is here. Why do you cry?" The woman did not seem very surprised. She straightened her appearance and thanked him, saying, "This is my good fortune." The scholar asked, "Why are you walking at night and weeping with such annoyance?" She said, "I am of the Gu family in the east village. Unfortunately, my husband passed away early, and I have been a widow. I served my mother-in-law, but because we did not get along, I was driven out by her."

First, it narrates Concubine Gu's favor while she was alive, then narrates her death, both of which serve as the foundation for the spiritual essence in the later text.

## The Spiritual Essence of Concubine Gu

During the reign of Emperor Guangzong of the Song dynasty, there was a favorite concubine surnamed Gu. She was selected to enter the palace. Her nature was agile, and she was quite knowledgeable about books and histories. She also dabbled in poetry and its laws. At the time, she was praised as virtuous within the palace. When she reached the end of her life, she was buried outside the Yongjin Gate in Hangzhou, on Magpie's Nest Mountain. Things changed and time moved on, and it became nothing more than a pile of mounds in the wilderness. By the second year of the Jingtai era, a scholar from Shanyin named Chen Yance, with the courtesy name Chengzhi, was also a man of wind and moon. He responded to the times, nourished things, and entrusted his boldness to the great mountains. He chanted to the moon and recited to the wind, keeping his secluded thoughts in poetry and rhyme. One day, he had business in Qiantang and lived abroad at Yongjin Gate. He thought of the journey to the Qiantang River and strolled, chanting a regulated poem:

> I have driven north and south, the years and months are distant;
> The traveler's feelings and thoughts are both far away.
> White sands and green bamboos on the wild riverbanks;
> Yellow reeds and water by the bridge outside the temple.
> A few sails cast toward the extreme shores; 
> Several sounds of the fisherman's flute rise with the cold tide;
> The state of the world is like floating clouds.

